<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/intermediate/muscles-and-energy-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Muscle is the body's engine, and the energy systems are <strong>three engines in one
    chassis</strong> — instantaneous, short-burst and long-haul — with the intensity dial
    setting the mix. You already know from the foundational lessons that movement
    happens when muscles pull on bones across joints; this page goes inside the engine
    room. How does a muscle cell actually make force? Where does the energy for a
    sprint, a 400 m, a marathon come from — and why do the answers explain training
    so precisely? This page covers the muscular system and its force machinery, the
    skeleton as a lever system (simple biomechanics), the ATP currency and the three
    pathways that recharge it, the fibre types that deal your genetic hand, and the
    effects of exercise on the body as the systems respond to load.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three engines, the overlap, training targets, fibre types" widget="equationStep" config={energyCfg} />

  <h2>The muscular system: how muscle makes force</h2>
  <p>
    Skeletal muscle is bundles within bundles: whole muscle, then fascicles, then
    muscle fibres (single giant cells), then <strong>myofibrils</strong>, and finally the
    contractile units — <strong>sarcomeres</strong> — laid end to end like train carriages.
    The sarcomere is where force happens: thick <strong>myosin</strong> filaments reach
    toward thin <strong>actin</strong> filaments, and when the nervous system commands a
    contraction, calcium is released, binding sites on the actin are exposed, and
    the myosin heads ratchet the filaments past each other — the <strong>sliding
    filament</strong> mechanism. The filaments do not shorten; they <em>slide</em>,
    pulling the Z-discs at each sarcomere's ends together, and summing across
    millions of sarcomeres is what shortens the whole muscle. Two further facts
    carry most of the practical weight. First, the <strong>motor unit</strong>: a
    motor neuron plus all the fibres it supplies (from a handful in the eye muscles
    to over a thousand in the quadriceps) fires <em>as one</em>, and force is graded
    by recruiting more units and firing them faster — small, fatigue-resistant
    units first, then bigger fast units as demand climbs. Second, muscles only
    <strong>pull</strong>: they cannot push, so every joint is served by antagonistic
    pairs — biceps flex the elbow while triceps extend it — and skilled movement
    is partly the nervous system's art of switching the pair members on and off.
  </p>

  <h2>The skeleton as a lever system: simple biomechanics</h2>
  <p>
    Bones are levers, joints are fulcrums, muscles supply the effort, and the load is
    whatever you are lifting (or your own limb). Physics recognises three lever
    classes, and the body contains all three: a rare <strong>first-class</strong> lever at
    the head nodding (joint between the effort of neck muscles and the load of the
    skull), the <strong>second-class</strong> lever of rising onto tiptoes (the ball of
    the foot is the fulcrum, body weight the load, the calf via the Achilles tendon
    the effort — effort arm longer than load arm, so this one has mechanical
    <em>advantage</em>), and — overwhelmingly — <strong>third-class</strong> levers, where the
    muscle inserts close to the joint. The biceps is the textbook case: it attaches
    a few centimetres from the elbow, while the load in the hand is maybe 35 cm
    away. The consequence is a deliberate mechanical <strong>disadvantage</strong>: to hold
    a 10 kg weight steady, the muscle must pull with roughly 70–90 kg of force —
    but the same short in-lever means the hand sweeps several times faster and
    further than the muscle shortens. The body is engineered for <em>speed and range
    at the cost of force</em>, which the muscles repay with cross-section: force in
    muscle is proportional to physiological cross-sectional area, which is why
    strength training builds girth and why the pennate (feathered) fibre
    arrangements of the calf and quad pack more fibres into the same limb
    volume. <strong>A worked number:</strong> the gastrocnemius–Achilles system lets a
    70 kg person rise onto one foot because the Achilles acts at a moment arm of
    roughly 5 cm behind the ankle while the body weight acts 20 cm in front — the
    calf must generate about 4× body weight ≈ 280 kg-force in a single-leg
    tiptoe, one reason the Achilles is the thickest tendon in the body.
  </p>

  <h2>ATP: the currency every engine spends</h2>
  <p>
    Every one of those myosin ratchets is paid in <strong>ATP</strong> (adenosine
    triphosphate): myosin binds ATP, splits it, and uses the released energy to
    pull; without ATP bound, the heads cannot detach — which is why rigor mortis
    is, at bottom, an ATP-supply failure. The striking fact is how little ATP the
    body stores: only about 80–100 g in total at any instant, enough for a couple
    of seconds of maximal effort. Performance is therefore not a storage problem
    but a <strong>recharging</strong> problem — and the three energy systems are three
    ways of regenerating ATP from ADP, differing in speed, capacity and fuel. The
    systems never take turns: all three run simultaneously, and <em>intensity</em>
    — not duration alone — decides which dominates the mix.
  </p>

  <h2>The phosphocreatine system: the first ten seconds</h2>
  <p>
    For explosive effort, muscle keeps a reserve fuel beside ATP itself:
    <strong>phosphocreatine (PCr)</strong>. The enzyme creatine kinase strips PCr's
    phosphate and bolts it directly onto ADP — a single-step reaction, needing
    no oxygen, delivering ATP within a fraction of a second. The capacity is
    small: muscle PCr can fuel roughly <strong>6–10 seconds of all-out work</strong>
    (a 100 m sprint, a maximal lift, a vault), then the tank is empty and needs
    3–5 minutes of rest to refill — which is why sprint training is done in short
    repeats with long recoveries, and why a powerlifter waits minutes between
    attempts. This system's practical importance made creatine the
    most-studied supplement in sports science: topping up the PCr pool with
    ~3–5 g of creatine monohydrate daily produces small but real gains in
    repeated high-intensity performance, with no credible harms at standard
    doses in healthy people.
  </p>

  <h2>The glycolytic system: sixty seconds of fire</h2>
  <p>
    For efforts from roughly 10 seconds to 2 minutes, muscle burns glucose — from
    blood sugar or its own stored <strong>glycogen</strong> — through
    <strong>anaerobic glycolysis</strong>: glucose is split to pyruvate, yielding ATP fast
    (2 ATP per glucose from muscle glycogen) but incompletely, and regenerating
    another currency (NAD⁺) the pathway needs to keep running. The fast way to
    do that is converting pyruvate to <strong>lactate</strong> — and here the textbook
    villain needs rehabilitation. The old story said lactic acid builds up,
    acidifies the muscle and causes fatigue and soreness. Modern exercise
    physiology (George Brooks's work above all) overturned it: lactate is
    produced <em>with</em> hydrogen ions, but the ions — the acidosis — are the
    burn's real cause, and <strong>lactate is a fuel</strong>, shuttled through the
    blood to be oxidised by the heart, the brain and neighbouring slow-twitch
    fibres within seconds to minutes (the <em>lactate shuttle</em>). Endurance
    training improves exactly this recycling: the trained athlete burns lactate
    as fast as they make it, so their <strong>lactate threshold</strong> — the
    exercise intensity above which blood lactate climbs persistently — sits at
    a higher fraction of their maximum. That threshold, measurable in the lab
    as blood lactate in mmol/L against workload, is among the best predictors
    of distance-running performance, better than raw VO₂max alone. The
    next-day soreness myth also dies here: soreness after novel exercise is
    <strong>DOMS</strong> — delayed-onset muscle soreness from microscopic structural
    damage and inflammation, not lactate, which is cleared within about an
    hour.
  </p>

  <h2>The aerobic system: the marathon engine</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond roughly two minutes, or at any lower intensity, ATP comes from
    <strong>oxidative phosphorylation</strong> in the mitochondria: fuels (glucose, fat,
    even lactate) are broken down completely — glycolysis, the Krebs cycle, the
    electron transport chain — yielding water, carbon dioxide and a rich ATP
    harvest (roughly 30–34 ATP per glucose versus 2 from anaerobic glycolysis).
    The rate is limited by oxygen delivery (the heart-and-lungs lesson's
    subject) and by mitochondrial density, which endurance training expands
    dramatically — more and larger mitochondria, more oxidative enzymes. Fuels
    trade off along the way: the <strong>crossover concept</strong> says fat supplies a
    larger share at low intensities (plentiful stores — tens of thousands of
    kcal of fat even in lean athletes — but slower to access, needing oxygen)
    while carbohydrate dominates as intensity rises (fast, and required once
    the effort outpaces oxygen supply). Carbohydrate stores are the bottleneck:
    muscle and liver glycogen total roughly 1,600–2,000 kcal, which is why
    "hitting the wall" in a marathon is a glycogen-depletion event around the
    30 km mark — and why the nutrition lesson's fuelling matters as much as
    the training.
  </p>

  <h2>Systems overlap: a 400 m, worked</h2>
  <p>
    The classic exam trap is treating the systems as time zones. They are a
    simultaneous blend. <strong>A worked example:</strong> the 400 m sprint (about 45–50
    seconds for a good athlete). Physiology's estimates put the energy mix at
    roughly 40–45% phosphocreatine-plus-glycolytic and 55–60% aerobic — a
    majority of the energy in one of track's most "anaerobic-feeling" events
    comes from oxygen-dependent pathways. The 100 m is roughly 85–90%
    anaerobic; the 1500 m about 80–85% aerobic; the marathon approximately
    99% aerobic. Each number shifts the training prescription: the 400 m
    runner needs the glycolytic engine <em>and</em> a serious aerobic base to
    speed lactate clearance between repeats — which is why modern 400 m
    programmes are not merely "sprint training". The systems also hand
    over <em>fatigue</em> differently: PCr depletion caps the sprint; acidosis
    caps the 60–90 second effort; glycogen, heat and central drive cap the
    long ones — and "the effects of exercise on the body" in the minutes
    after a session (elevated breathing, oxygen debt formally called EPOC,
    elevated temperature) are the systems' repair bills being paid.
  </p>

  <h2>Fibre types: the genetic hand</h2>
  <p>
    Muscles are mixtures of fibre types, and the mix is largely genetic.
    <strong>Type I</strong> ("slow-twitch") contracts slowly, is rich in mitochondria and
    myoglobin (hence its red colour), resists fatigue superbly and powers
    posture and distance running. <strong>Type IIx</strong> ("fast-twitch") contracts fast
    and hard, is pale and glycolytic, fatigues quickly and powers sprints and
    jumps. <strong>Type IIa</strong> sits between — fast and trainable toward endurance
    properties. Biopsies of elite athletes show the selection: champion
    sprinters carry roughly 70–80% fast-twitch fibres; elite distance runners
    show the mirror image. Training shifts properties within the fast family
    (IIx → IIa with endurance work — a real but modest conversion) and grows
    the size and capacity of whatever fibres exist; it cannot convert Type I
    into Type II. The teaching point for the athlete and the coach: test the
    natural hand (jump-and-sprint tests predict fibre profile reasonably),
    then choose events and training where that hand wins.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fatigue is not one thing.</strong> Peripheral fatigue lives in the muscle —
    metabolite accumulation, reduced calcium release, PCr depletion. Central
    fatigue lives in the nervous system — the brain's Protective regulation of
    effort (the "central governor" debate, still contested) and spinal
    reflexes that throttle recruitment. The evidence says both matter and mix
    task-dependently: heat stress exaggerates the central component; a
    maximal lift is more peripheral. That is why pacing, motivation and
    cooling are performance variables, not just comfort.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Training is the systems' dose-response.</strong> Sprint work upregulates
    the phosphagen enzymes and neural drive; interval training (4–6 × 30 s
    all-out, the classic protocol) measurably boosts glycolytic capacity and
    lactate shuttling within weeks; long steady work builds mitochondria,
    capillaries and fat-oxidation machinery. The specificity principle is a
    molecular statement: you become the demands you place on the tissue.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How much of the elite fibre profile is
    developmental (early specialisation selects and trains the profile) versus
    hard genetic lottery remains unresolved; the ACE and ACTN3 genes are
    markers, not destiny. And the extreme-duration question — how ultra
    athletes fuel 100-mile efforts when glycogen is exhausted — is rewriting
    the fat-adaptation literature in real time.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brooks on lactate and the lactate shuttle; McArdle, Katch &amp; Katch, Exercise Physiology; the biology lesson's respiration chapter.</p>

